.TH NAP 3 LOCAL
.SH NAME
nap, naphz \- sleep for a fraction of a second
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <nap.h>
.PP
.B nap(ticks)
.PP
.B naphz()
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Nap
suspends the calling process for
.I ticks
sixtieths of a second.
.I Naphz
returns the number of ticks in one second, as the kernel counts them
.RI ( HZ ,
60 on this machine); divide by it to convert, and do not assume the
value.
.PP
Both open
.B /dev/nap
on first use and keep the descriptor.  The descriptor has the
close-on-exec bit set, so it is not inherited across
.IR exec .
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
Zero on success, \-1 with
.I errno
set on error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.SM ENXIO
.B /dev/nap
is missing, or the driver is not in the running kernel.
.TP
.SM EINVAL
.I ticks
is negative or exceeds
.SM NAPMAXT
(two seconds' worth).  Use
.IR sleep (3)
for longer delays.
.TP
.SM EAGAIN
Too many processes are napping at once (see
.SM NNAP
in the driver).  Retry.
.TP
.SM EMFILE
No descriptor was available for
.BR /dev/nap .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sleep(3), alarm(2), time(2) for
.IR ftime ,
which measures sub-second time but cannot wait for it
.SH BUGS
A nap cannot be interrupted by a signal; the signal is delivered when
the nap ends.  This is deliberate \- V7 has no
.IR untimeout ()
with which to cancel the pending callout \- and is why the maximum nap
is short.
.PP
.I Nap
rounds nothing: it waits between
.I ticks\-1
and
.I ticks
ticks, because the current tick is already partly spent when the call
is made.  Ask for one more tick than you need if the floor matters.
